# Prompt: add the "Screen Pad" Mobile Controls scheme to ALVA

You are working in the `hiyroscript/alva` repository. Read `CLAUDE.md` first and follow it, plus `ALVA_SPEC.md` §6.10 / §6.10a / §7.4, `docs/systems/input.md` (the "Touch controls" section especially), `docs/systems/movement.md`, `codename_rule`, and `docs/characters/adding-characters.md#the-touch-comfort-rules-strict`. Never edit `character_rule`. Run `node --test` before and after; everything must stay green.

## Goal

Add a third Mobile Controls scheme, **Screen Pad** (French: **Pavé tactile**; settings id `screenpad`), next to **Joystick** and **Classic Buttons**, and make it **the new default** (see "Default scheme" below). In Screen Pad, the lower-left cluster disappears entirely: no joystick, no Run arrows, no Left/Right mouvement buttons, no Down button. Instead, **the left half of the screen is an invisible gesture surface for movement**, and **the right half keeps the existing lower-right action cluster exactly as it is today** (Mash Combo pair + Jump in the corner, Shield above Jump, the fighter's other moves in columns above, same sizes, same art, same live cooldown/spent states).

Screen Pad must produce the same control codenames and snapshot fields as every other device (`runLeft`, `runRight`, `down`, `jump`, `mouvementLeft` / `mouvementRight` via `queueTouchMouvement`, with their `…Pressed` edges). The Fighter, the movement rules, `js/data/movement.js`, Online Play's `encodeFrame`, the CPU and the controller code must not change at all. If something looks like it needs a Fighter change, stop and reconsider: the gesture layer should translate into existing inputs.

## The gestures (left half)

**The pad covers the whole left half of the screen**: every pixel from the left edge to the horizontal midpoint, top to bottom, the safe-area insets and the area under the top HUD included (the HUD is display only and must not swallow touches there: give it `pointer-events: none` over the pad). The only things on the left half that take a touch before the pad are the pause button and any right-cluster button a player has moved there in the editor. Size it from the live viewport (resize and orientation changes included), never from a fixed box. Each pointer that goes down in it is a **gesture pointer**. Only one gesture pointer is active at a time; a second finger landing on the left half while one is active is ignored (except for the Super Jump chord below). Fingers on the right-hand buttons are fully independent (multi-touch, as today).

All thresholds are in CSS pixels scaled by the viewport's short side (define them as a fraction, e.g. `0.06 * shortSide`, so phones and tablets feel the same), and live in one frozen `SCREEN_PAD` object in `js/ui/touch-controls.js` (or a new `js/ui/screen-pad.js`), next to `JOYSTICK`, with pure functions you can unit-test, like `joystickDirection` / `joystickVertical`.

| Gesture | Result | Codename(s) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Tap** (down and up within `tapTime` ≈ 0.20 s, moved less than `tapSlop` ≈ 3 % of the short side) | Jump. In the air, an air jump (so tap-tap-tap = the triple jump). | one `jump` press (held for one step, then let go) |
| **Swipe left / right** (lifted within `swipeTime` ≈ 0.25 s, travelled past `swipeDistance` ≈ 8 % of short side, horizontal dominant: within 35° of horizontal) | Dash on the ground, air dash in the air, toward the swipe. | `queueTouchMouvement('left' \| 'right')` exactly once |
| **Touch, hold and drag sideways** (a *floating stick*: the anchor is where the finger landed) | Run left / right for as long as the finger is held past the deadzone. Reuse `joystickDirection`'s engage/deadzone hysteresis, with distance measured from the anchor. | `runLeft` / `runRight` held |
| **Drag down while holding** (past `downEngage`, within the down angle) | Down: fast fall in the air; held on the ground it arms the Super Jump. Reuse `joystickVertical`'s engage/release/angle hysteresis. A diagonal holds a Run and Down together, as the joystick does. | `down` held |
| **Quick flick down** (down past threshold and lifted within `dropTapTime`) | Drop through a one-way platform. Falls out naturally: it is a short `down` tap, exactly the joystick's flick. In the air it is a short fast fall. | short `down` hold |
| **Swipe up** (fast, vertical dominant, lifted within `swipeTime`) | **Super Jump** on the ground (a normal jump in the air). Emit `down` held and `jump` pressed **in the same sample**, then release both: the Fighter already treats Down + fresh Jump on the same sample as the Super Jump (`movement.md`). | `down` + `jump` same sample |
| **Hold down on the pad + tap Jump on the right** | Also the Super Jump: the existing chord, unchanged. | `down` held + right Jump |

The floating stick re-anchors if the finger drifts far beyond `maxRadius` (drag the anchor along, like a mobile-game floating joystick), so a long slide never "runs out" of stick.

### Disambiguation rules (the important part)

1. **Jump fires on lift, not on touch-down.** Otherwise every run would start with a jump. To keep it responsive, emit the jump the moment the tap is recognised (on `pointerup`), never wait for a possible double-tap.
2. **A gesture is classified once.** On lift: tap → swipe (left/right/up) → nothing. Once a pointer has started holding a Run or Down (dragged past the stick's engage distance *and* held past `tapTime`), it is a stick: lifting it never also jumps or dashes.
3. **Swipes may briefly run first.** A fast swipe may cross the Run engage distance before it is recognised; that is fine (a few frames of `runLeft` before the Dash in the same direction). A swipe *opposite* to the held Run is still a Dash that way.
4. **Mid-hold flick = Dash (optional, behind a constant, default ON):** while holding a Run, a sudden horizontal velocity spike past `flickSpeed` (≈ 1.5 short-sides / second) in either direction queues one Dash that way and re-anchors the stick at the finger, so the player can run → dash → keep running without lifting. One Dash per spike; the stick must slow below half `flickSpeed` before another can fire.
5. **Pushing up while holding** does nothing (same as the Joystick scheme): Jump is a tap, so a drifting thumb never jumps.
6. Opposing Dash requests in one step cancel, as everywhere else.

### Invisible at all times

The pad is **always invisible**: no ring, knob, anchor, trail, swipe streak, divider, outline, tint or pressed state, ever, in play, in pause, in Practice Ground and in the touch layout editor. It is a transparent element (`background: transparent`, no border, no shadow, no `::before` / `::after` art) that only receives pointers. Nothing about it is drawn when a gesture is recognised; the fighter's own movement is the feedback. It must not be announced as a button to assistive technology (`aria-hidden="true"`); keyboard and controller play are unaffected.

The only text that may ever mention it is outside the pad: the Settings card's description, and a one-time hint card on the first battle with Screen Pad ("Left half of the screen — Tap: jump · Swipe: dash · Hold & drag: move · Drag down: down · Swipe up: Super Jump"), dismissed by any touch, remembered in `settings.js` (the only module touching storage). The hint is a card, not a drawing of the pad.

## The right half

Unchanged: same buttons, sizes (130 % / 110 %), positions, art, `showStatus`, Mash Combo row, Shield/Deflect, and the right-hand **Jump button stays** (the touch comfort rule requires Jump in the corner beside the Mash pair, and it gives players a second jump and the Down + Jump chord). Taps on any right-hand button never count as left-zone gestures, even if a button sits left of the midpoint after customization: buttons always win over the pad (stop propagation at the button, as the Down button already does for Classic's sliding container).

